--- body ---
## Scope

After technical validation works, decide which curated entries are exposed and
which one is the default. R7 and R29 conformance is the first filter and it is
not traded against anything (the author's rulings, `DECISIONS.md`
2026-08-31 -- the swap is a conformance repair, not a move on a
cost-versus-quality frontier). An entry whose declared language set is not
multilingual is not a candidate for the default, whatever it scores; nor is one
that cannot cross languages, which is measured rather than declared -- ticket
0266's cross-lingual arm at the deployed dtype, both controls, is the evidence
this filter reads. The private golden retrieval gate and the published resource
budgets then choose among the entries that pass that filter. Compatibility crowd counts are supporting operability
evidence only.

## Exit criteria

- Every proposed shipped entry passes local compatibility, the golden retrieval
  gate and resource limits on its supported shapes.
- Licences, artifact sizes and runtime support are disclosed beside the entry.
- The decision and its negative controls are recorded in DECISIONS.md.
- The entry proposed as the default is R7-conformant, read from its declared
  language set rather than from its name or its scores, and R29-conformant on
  ticket 0266's measured cross-lingual arm including its negative control.
- If no entry passes, MiniLM stays in place as the disclosed interim state of a
  known non-conformance -- never as a finding that the evidence did not justify
  a change -- and the ticket names which gate blocked which entry.
